{"message":"I appreciate you starting plainly, so let me do the same. FF did not fight this election on a muddled centre — we fought it on rebuilding public health, defending pensions, and pulling the economy back from the market logic your voters want more of. A 'firmly market-oriented' economic platform with a binding spending cap is not something I can sell to my people as a compromise; it's a surrender, and they would see it as one within a week. If you want FF in cabinet, the platform needs to sit clearly left of where you're describing — I'd need economic policy at -2 or better on the scale we both know, and health provision meaningfully public, not opened to the market. A spending cap that ties our hands on pensions or health funding is a non-starter regardless of what else is on the table.

On law and order I'm less rigid — I didn't campaign hard on that axis and there's room to meet you partway on toughness without it costing me anything at home. Environment, immigration, transport, social policy — as you say, there's genuine flexibility there and I don't think that's where this negotiation lives or dies.

So here's the honest fork: if you want FF's 132 seats inside government, the economic and health lines have to move substantially toward us, and pensions are untouchable — full stop. If that's further than you're willing to go, I could instead consider staying outside cabinet and simply not voting you down, but that costs less because I'd be conceding less — I'd still need the platform at economic zero or better, and the same pension guarantee. Tell me which government you're actually trying to build, because the one you just described isn't one I can put my name to.","ending":"continue"}
